## Why This Is Asked
Tests memory ownership, cgo boundaries, and concurrency safety by wrapping a C-allocated string.

## Key Concepts
- CGO memory management
- C->Go string conversion
- Error propagation across FFI
- Concurrent Go calls without shared mutable state

## Code Example
```c
/* header: cgoc_hello.h */
#ifndef CGOA_HELLO_H
#define CGOA_HELLO_H
#ifdef __cplusplus
extern "C" {
#endif
unsigned char* greet(const char* name, int* err); // returns malloc'ed buffer or NULL
#ifdef __cplusplus
}
#endif
#endif
```

```c
/* minimal implementation: cg_hello.c */
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include "cgoc_hello.h"
unsigned char* greet(const char* name, int* err){
  if(!name){ *err = 1; return NULL; }
  const char* base = "Hello, ";
  size_t len = strlen(base) + strlen(name) + 2;
  char* out = (char*)malloc(len);
  if(!out){ *err = 2; return NULL; }
  strcpy(out, base);
  strcat(out, name);
  *err = 0; return (unsigned char*)out;
}
```

```go
// go binding: cg_hello.go
package cgHello
/*
#include "cgoc_hello.h"
#include <stdlib.h>
*/
import "C"
import (
  "errors"
  "unsafe"
)

func GetGreeting(name string) (string, error) {
  cname := C.CString(name)
  defer C.free(unsafe.Pointer(cname))
  var err C.int
  buf := C.greet(cname, &err)
  if buf == nil {
    switch err {
    default:
      return "", errors.New("greet failed")
    }
  }
  defer C.free(unsafe.Pointer(buf))
  goStr := C.GoString((*C.char)(unsafe.Pointer(buf)))
  return goStr, nil
}
```

```go
// main.go
package main
import (
  "fmt"
  cg "path/to/cgHello"
  "sync"
)
func main(){
  var wg sync.WaitGroup
  names := []string{"Alice","Bob"}
  for _, n := range names {
    wg.Add(1)
    go func(name string){
      defer wg.Done()
      s, err := cg.GetGreeting(name)
      if err != nil { fmt.Println("err", err); return }
      fmt.Println(s)
    }(n)
  }
  wg.Wait()
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to a streaming callback (callback into Go) safely?
- How would you handle error codes with richer semantics and avoid leaking memory if multiple errors occur?

